---
import ReturnHome from "$components/ReturnHome.astro"
import Layout from "$layouts/Layout.astro"

/**
 * Personal page about the habits I try to maintain to stay healthy.
 */
const pageTitle = "My health"
---

<Layout title={pageTitle}>
	<section>
		<h1>{pageTitle}</h1>
		<p>
			Some tools and thoughts to maintain good habits. I don't want to obsess
			over my health, but I do want to stay healthy enough to fully enjoy life.
		</p>
		<ReturnHome />
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2 id="the_basics">The basics</h2>
		<p>
			Based on the strongest scientific literature, there are several habits
			that can significantly improve my health: a balanced diet, daily physical
			activity, regular sleep schedules,<sup>1</sup> no tobacco,<sup>2</sup> little
			to none alcohol,<sup>3</sup> avoiding air pollution,<sup>4</sup> reducing UV
			exposure,<sup>5</sup> and quality social relationships.<sup>6</sup
			><side-note class="xl:-mt-16"
				>1 – Regularity predicts mortality better than duration, with the most
				regular sleepers showing 20 to 48% lower all-cause mortality than the
				least regular ones ☞ Windred DP & al (2024). <i
					>Sleep regularity is a stronger predictor of mortality risk than sleep
					duration</i
				>. Sleep 47(1) <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37738616/"
					>Source</a
				></side-note
			><side-note
				>2 – Smokers lose at least a decade of life expectancy, and quitting
				before 40 removes about 90% of that excess risk ☞ Jha P & al (2013). <i
					>21st-century hazards of smoking and benefits of cessation in the
					United States</i
				>. N Engl J Med 368(4) <a
					href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23343063/">Source</a
				></side-note
			><side-note
				>3 – Once the abstainer bias is corrected, the supposed benefit of
				moderate drinking disappears. Worth knowing that the <abbr
					title="National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine"
					>NASEM</abbr
				> 2025 review concludes the opposite, so the debate is still open ☞ Zhao J
				& al (2023). <i
					>Association between daily alcohol intake and risk of all-cause
					mortality</i
				>. <abbr>JAMA</abbr> Netw Open 6(3) <a
					href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37000449/">Source</a
				></side-note
			><side-note
				>4 – I currently live in Toulouse, France, which has a good to moderate
				air quality. But I plan to move to Helsinki, Finland, in the near
				future, which has excellent to good air quality. Either way it is worth
				the attention, as an extra 5 µg/m³ of <abbr
					title="Fine particulate matter under 2.5 micrometres">PM2.5</abbr
				> raises natural-cause mortality by 13%, and the effect holds below the regulatory
				limits ☞ Strak M & al (2021). <i
					>Long term exposure to low level air pollution and mortality in eight
					European cohorts within the <abbr>ELAPSE</abbr> project</i
				>. <abbr title="British Medical Journal">BMJ</abbr> 374 <a
					href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34470785/">Source</a
				></side-note
			><side-note
				>5 – In the only randomised trial on the question, daily sunscreen use
				left about a quarter as many invasive melanomas after ten years of
				follow-up (<abbr title="Hazard Ratio">HR</abbr> 0.27) ☞ Green AC & al (2011).
				<i
					>Reduced melanoma after regular sunscreen use: randomized trial
					follow-up</i
				>. J Clin Oncol 29(3) <a
					href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21135266/">Source</a
				></side-note
			><side-note
				>6 – The <abbr title="World Health Organization">WHO</abbr> links around 871,000
				deaths a year to loneliness and social isolation, and a meta-analysis of 148
				studies puts the survival advantage of good social relationships at 50% ☞
				<abbr title="World Health Organization">WHO</abbr> Commission on Social Connection
				(2025). <i
					>From loneliness to social connection: charting a path to healthier
					societies</i
				>
				<a
					href="https://www.who.int/groups/commission-on-social-connection/report/"
					>Source</a
				>; Holt-Lunstad J & al (2010). <i
					>Social relationships and mortality risk: a meta-analytic review</i
				>. <abbr title="Public Library of Science">PLOS</abbr> Med 7(7) <a
					href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20668659/">Source</a
				></side-note
			>
		</p>
		<p>
			Everything else tends to be marginal, less proven, not actionable, or
			irrelevant for me, when it isn't an outright marketing scam.
		</p>
		<p>
			What I mean by a balanced diet is a diverse and mostly plant-based one,
			with enough protein, fibre, and healthy fats. I don't want to avoid all
			salty, processed, and sugary foods as long as they stay occasional, and
			even less to follow strict diets that are hard to maintain,
			guilt-inducing, and often not backed by science. To help me get there, I
			first leaned on frozen ready vegetables, then on HelloFresh (more variety
			and the pleasure of cooking, but for a higher price) and I keep a water
			bottle at hand for hydration.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2 id="physical_activity">Physical activity</h2>
		<p>
			I got back into training in 2026 with a single goal, long-term health.
			Most of the mortality benefit of muscle strengthening is captured in the
			first hour a week,<sup>7</sup> and the dose-response curve flattens fast: the
			frequency and volume that separate a decent programme from an optimal one barely
			matter when I chase neither performance nor looks.<sup>8</sup> My constraint
			has never been the stimulus, it has always been consistency.<side-note
				>7 – Maximum risk reduction lands at roughly 30 to 60 minutes of muscle
				strengthening a week. The curve rising again beyond that is most likely
				an artefact of self-reported data, and is no reason to cap volume ☞
				Momma H & al (2022). <i
					>Muscle-strengthening activities are associated with lower risk and
					mortality in major non-communicable diseases: a systematic review and
					meta-analysis of cohort studies</i
				>. Br J Sports Med 56(13) <a
					href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35228201/">Source</a
				></side-note
			><side-note
				>8 – At equated volume, frequency has a negligible effect on muscle
				growth, volume shows sharply diminishing returns, and most of the
				strength gain is already there at two sessions a week ☞ Pelland JC & al
				(2026). <i
					>The resistance training dose response: meta-regressions exploring the
					effects of weekly volume and frequency on muscle hypertrophy and
					strength gains</i
				>. Sports Med 56 <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41343037/"
					>Source</a
				></side-note
			>
		</p>
		<p>
			In practice, a home base for cardio, core, and mobility (Darebee's Drifter
			programme), plus two weekly strength sessions at the gym around loaded
			compound movements (squat, deadlift, presses, and rows). Always two or
			three reps in reserve, never to failure, which costs almost nothing and
			buys recovery and technical margin.<sup>9</sup> Progression is written in advance,
			so nothing depends on the mood of the day. The one thing I don't compromise
			on is the intensity of the main lifts, as bone responds to load magnitude rather
			than to volume or modality, and light exercise is inert for it.<sup
				>10</sup
			><side-note
				>9 – Proximity to failure has no detectable effect on strength gains and
				a trivial one on hypertrophy ☞ Robinson ZP & al (2024). <i
					>Exploring the dose-response relationship between estimated resistance
					training proximity to failure, strength gain, and muscle hypertrophy:
					a series of meta-regressions</i
				>. Sports Med 54(9) <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38970765/"
					>Source</a
				>; Refalo MC & al (2023). <i
					>Influence of resistance training proximity-to-failure on skeletal
					muscle hypertrophy: a systematic review with meta-analysis</i
				>. Sports Med 53(3) <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36334240/"
					>Source</a
				></side-note
			><side-note
				>10 – Intensity is the discriminant. Low-intensity protocols are without
				effect, and heavy resistance combined with impact is the most osteogenic
				stimulus ☞ Kistler-Fischbacher M & al (2021). <i
					>The effect of exercise intensity on bone in postmenopausal women
					(part 2): a meta-analysis</i
				>. Bone 143 <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33357834/"
					>Source</a
				>; Watson SL & al (2018). <i
					>High-intensity resistance and impact training improves bone mineral
					density and physical function in postmenopausal women with osteopenia
					and osteoporosis: the LIFTMOR randomized controlled trial</i
				>. J Bone Miner Res 33(2) <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/jbmr.3284"
					>Source</a
				></side-note
			>
		</p>
		<p>
			The most useful step was auditing all of it against the primary literature
			rather than the fitness media consensus. A good half of what gets argued
			over online (free weights against machines, this progression method, that
			piece of kit) has no measurable effect at twelve months. My expectations
			follow: nothing visible before two or three months, early gains that are
			mostly neural, and on bone, no perceptible feedback at all. It is a
			deliberate bet on a mechanism, not on a sensation.
		</p>
		<p>
			The programme runs on an automated tracker and planner at (work in
			progress) <a href="https://body.goulven-clech.dev"
				>body.goulven-clech.dev</a
			> which also keeps a simple sleep log and holds me to a floor of 6,000 steps
			a day, addressing separate but equally important problems.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2 id="mind_activity">Mind activity</h2>
		<draft-section>
			<p>
				TODO: my media diet, my absence from social networks, and my continuous
				learning programme.
			</p>
		</draft-section>
	</section>
</Layout>
